<h2>Why a trenchless company still digs</h2>
<p>We built our business on <a href="/cipp-pipe-lining/">lining</a> and <a href="/pipe-bursting/">bursting</a>, and we recommend them whenever the pipe allows. But both methods have limits, and Colorado soil finds them. Expansive clay heaves and settles for decades, and a sewer running through it can end up with low spots, reversed slope, or sections that have moved sideways. Methods that follow the existing path cannot fix the path itself.</p>
<p>When the camera shows that kind of damage, the right repair is to open the ground, set the pipe on proper bedding at the correct slope, and close it up again. Saying so plainly is part of the job.</p>

<h2>Spot repair: fix the section that failed</h2>
<p>Many lines are sound except for one bad stretch. A root-heaved joint near a tree, a broken section under an old patch, or a short sag where the soil settled. In those cases we excavate at that one location, cut out the damaged piece, and replace it with new pipe on compacted bedding at the right grade.</p>
<p>A spot repair keeps the dig small. It is often combined with lining: we fix the section that lining could not handle, then line the remainder from that opening or from a cleanout. On a builder's project in Arvada we repaired one section of a 36-foot cast iron line and lined the rest through to the city main. That hybrid is frequently the least disruptive correct answer.</p>

<h2>Full replacement: when the whole line needs it</h2>
<p>Some lines are beyond piecemeal fixes. We recommend conventional replacement when:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The line has lost grade over much of its length.</strong> Water stands or flows backward in long stretches, and no in-place method will change that.</li>
  <li><strong>There are multiple bellies.</strong> A series of sags from settling soil means the bedding under the pipe has failed along the run.</li>
  <li><strong>The pipe has collapsed in several places.</strong> Bursting needs a continuous path; lining needs a host pipe.</li>
  <li><strong>The route has to change.</strong> An addition was built over the old line, the connection point is moving, or the line conflicts with other utilities.</li>
  <li><strong>Bursting is ruled out.</strong> The line is too close to a foundation or other utilities, or sits in shallow bedrock in a foothill neighborhood.</li>
  <li><strong>The line froze and split.</strong> Usually because it was laid too shallow; replacement lets us set it at a proper depth.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How we do it</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Camera and locate.</strong> We confirm the route, depth and the exact extent of the damage so the excavation is no bigger than needed.</li>
  <li><strong>Utility locates and permits.</strong> Utilities are marked before any digging, and we handle the permits required by your city or special sanitation district, including right-of-way or street-cut permits when the work reaches the road.</li>
  <li><strong>Excavation and shoring.</strong> Colorado laterals run below frost depth, so trenches can be deep. We shore them as conditions require.</li>
  <li><strong>New pipe on proper bedding.</strong> The new line is set on compacted material at a steady slope toward the main, with cleanouts where they belong.</li>
  <li><strong>Inspection.</strong> Where the city or district requires an open-trench inspection, we schedule it before backfill.</li>
  <li><strong>Backfill and compaction.</strong> Soil goes back in layers and is compacted so the surface does not settle later.</li>
  <li><strong>Final camera run.</strong> You get video of the finished line.</li>
</ol>

<h2>The street, the tap and local rules</h2>
<p>In most Front Range cities and districts, owners are responsible for the lateral from the house to the main, including the tap and the section under the street. When that part has failed, a replacement can mean cutting pavement, traffic control and restoring the street to the city's standard. Where possible, we avoid the street cut by bursting or lining that section and digging only on private property.</p>
<p>The Denver metro is a patchwork of city utilities and special sanitation districts, each with its own permits, pipe and bedding specifications, and inspection steps. We confirm what applies to your address before we book the work.</p>

<h2>Restoring what we open</h2>
<p>Every excavation estimate includes what goes back on top: sod, landscaping, walks, or concrete. We plan the dig around what matters to you, whether that is a mature tree, a newly poured patio or a finished basement, and we tell you in advance what will be disturbed and for how long.</p>

<h2>Getting a straight answer</h2>
<p>If you have been told you need a full dig, we are happy to look at the line and give a second opinion. Sometimes a spot repair plus lining will do. Sometimes the first contractor was right. Either way, you will see the footage and hear the reasoning. Read more about <a href="/our-process/">our process</a>, the broader range of <a href="/trenchless-sewer-repair/">trenchless options</a>, or our <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>.</p>
